\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 The destruction of part of Rotherhithe began, as many of humanity\'92s more durable disasters have, with a group of people deciding that something they loved had become too commercial.\
In this case the thing they loved was quake-trance, a form of music which had spent the better part of a century persuading the American Southwest to participate in outdoor festivals against its geological better judgement.\
Quake-trance was descended from psychedelic trance, although by the time anybody started keeping reliable records of it, several centuries of genre mutation had rendered the family resemblance argumentative. Progressive psy had contributed the rolling pulse and the patience to spend twenty minutes going somewhere. Darker forms of psytrance supplied the suspicion that wherever the music was going might contain teeth. Forest psy contributed the belief that the environment ought to be involved, an idea quake-trance improved upon by asking why anyone should waste time sampling nature when nature could be made to perform live.\
Hi-tech psytrance had taken the opposite evolutionary path and spent generations making itself faster. Quake-trance became more extreme by heading downwards.\
A typical set might move along at a perfectly danceable tempo while a second musical structure developed beneath the range of normal hearing. The dancers got the kick and bass. Gyges discs, salvaged from obsolete industrial stasis equipment and operated with degrees of legality varying according to distance from the nearest road, supplied frequencies which expressed themselves more persuasively through feet, ribs, loose masonry and parked vehicles.\
People danced to the beat.\
The landscape got the remainder.\
Nobody planned the genre. The first practitioners were people with very large sound systems, access to discarded industrial machinery and the conviction that the phrase 
\f1\i manufacturer\'92s operating limits
\f0\i0  represented the beginning of a negotiation.\
The American Southwest attracted precisely that sort of person.\
By the early twenty-third century, the country around Joshua Tree, Yucca Valley and Twentynine Palms contained a healthy population of musicians, artists, engineers who had ceased being invited to professional conferences, and people whose occupations became less convincing each time they explained them. The desert offered cheap space, forgiving neighbours by virtue of having very few neighbours, and exposed geology capable of providing immediate criticism.\
Dry rock turned out to be particularly good at this.\
Put a conventional bass system into a canyon and people heard bass.\
Add a spinning Gyges assembly at the right frequency and the canyon acquired opinions.\
The earliest stories describe dust falling from rock faces, pebbles hopping against gravity for fractions of a second, aluminium tent poles humming in sympathy and, during one particularly well-attested performance, three parked camper vans moving approximately eleven centimetres north while every person responsible for them was elsewhere insisting the parking brakes were on.\
Quake-trance enthusiasts came to call the moment when the surroundings joined in 
\f2\b the Drop
\f0\b0 .\
The term was inherited from ancient electronic dance music, where it seems to have meant a moment at which a piece of music resumed after briefly pretending it might not. Quake-trance musicians found this insufficiently ambitious.\
A Drop had occurred when the environment became part of the performance.\
A club floor vibrating under a bass stack did not count, because club floors were known collaborators.\
A festival stage moving was respectable.\
Loose stones joining the rhythm were better.\
A canyon wall humming qualified as serious work.\
A Drop which produced correspondence from the Geological Survey could be discussed for years.\
Naturally, everyone later disagreed about where the first proper one happened.\
Joshua Tree claimed the honour with the confidence of somewhere that already had enough mythology to absorb another piece. According to its preferred account, two obsolete Gyges units had been attached to a desert sound system during an illegal party beyond Twentynine Palms because the DJ wanted \'93more bottom\'94. Midway through a long progressive set, one unit drifted out of its intended operating regime, the second followed, and a sandstone face eighty metres away began humming loudly enough for the crowd to stop dancing.\
The DJ did the one thing upon which every version of the story agrees.\
He kept playing.\
He adjusted the tempo until the wall got louder.\
People put their palms against the stone and felt the pulse coming back through it.\
Someone said the canyon was breathing.\
Someone else said Rickenbacker was listening.\
Within six months there were T-shirts.\
The original party invitation survived much longer than any useful directions:\
DRIVE EAST UNTIL YOUR NAV STOPS AGREEING WITH ITSELF.\
TURN LEFT AT THE BURNT SOFA.\
For the next hundred years, pilgrims argued about which burnt sofa had been meant. Eventually there were seven candidate sites and considerably more than seven burnt sofas, several of which had been placed deliberately by landowners who understood religious tourism.\
Slab City rejected the Joshua Tree story completely.\
Its adherents credited a technician remembered only as Manny No-Last-Name, who had supposedly connected a surplus Gyges regulator to a bass stack after declaring that conventional subwoofers were \'93for indoor people\'94. Depending on the source, Manny was a gifted engineer, a fugitive refrigeration mechanic, three brothers sharing one identity, or a convenient name used by anybody who had rewired something while drunk.\
The later Order of the Perpetual Drop rejected Manny on doctrinal grounds, principally because nobody could establish whether he had existed in the singular.\
Arcosanti entered the dispute later and made the unforgivable contribution of understanding some of it.\
Researchers there noticed that particular environmental responses changed with the geometry and relative timing of multiple stasis fields. Musicians who had been content to make cliffs vibrate found themselves sharing desert camps with physicists carrying instruments and asking them to reproduce things.\
The resulting school called its practitioners 
\f2\b geo-composers
\f0\b0 .\
Everyone else called them worse things.\
Arcosanti nevertheless became the intellectual centre of quake-trance. Joshua Tree supplied the mysticism, Slab City claimed to have supplied the dangerous bit, and Arcosanti supplied equations which proved that both groups had accidentally discovered something more interesting than either of them understood.\
Around the same period, the musical jokes concerning Ludwig, Zildjian and Rickenbacker began hardening into theology.\
The names were ancient.\
The Great Silicon Meltdown of the mid-twenty-first century had taken an enormous bite out of American cultural memory when a cascading series of infrastructure failures boiled major data centres across the Lower States into extremely expensive sludge. Films vanished. Music vanished. Books, letters, photographs, recordings and vast quantities of family history disappeared.\
Financial records had naturally been replicated elsewhere.\
Physical objects survived rather better than their explanations.\
Centuries later, archaeomusicologists repeatedly encountered the names 
\f2\b LUDWIG
\f0\b0 , 
\f2\b ZILDJIAN
\f0\b0  and 
\f2\b RICKENBACKER
\f0\b0 on artefacts clearly associated with mass musical ceremonies. There were drums bearing Ludwig\'92s name, bronze discs marked Zildjian, and long-necked electrical instruments carrying the name Rickenbacker. Surviving images showed these objects being handled upon raised, illuminated platforms before thousands of people displaying the physical symptoms of collective ecstasy.\
A few scholars proposed that the three names belonged to commercial manufacturers.\
The theory explained almost everything and therefore had very little chance.\
Quake-trance already had slang uses for the names. Ludwig was invoked when the kick landed particularly hard. Zildjian was blamed when metalwork began rattling. Rickenbacker was said to be listening whenever the sub-bass travelled unusually well through the ground.\
At first everyone understood these as jokes.\
Then a canyon answered.\
The precise evening is disputed, along with almost everything worth disputing in quake-trance history, but several accounts describe a Drop during which the rock continued resonating after the audible music had fallen away. Someone shouted that Rickenbacker was still listening.\
People laughed.\
For the next twenty years they kept saying it.\
After fifty years it was traditional.\
Someone eventually wrote it down.\
Someone else wrote explanatory notes.\
The notes survived longer than the joke.\
Ludwig became the god of impact, pulse, thunder and the First Beat, the force by which vibration entered the world. Zildjian acquired the breaking sky, fracture, metallic violence and the sacred Crash, which marked any transition violent enough to threaten equipment.\
Rickenbacker took everything beneath.\
Bass belonged to him, along with stone, buried vibration, sympathetic resonance and messages which could be felt long before they could be heard.\
He became the most important of the three because quake-trance was increasingly concerned with what happened after the sound entered the ground.\
The most widely accepted doctrine held that Rickenbacker did not travel through the earth. He was already underneath every place.\
This later annoyed physicists enormously.\
Pearl, Gibson and H\'f6fner survived as minor gods from older versions of the faith. Pearl had once fought Ludwig during the Great Drum Schism, a dispute whose surviving texts showed both parties defending the sacred primacy of drums. Gibson acquired flamboyance, solos, heroic excess and the ancient rite of continuing to play after everyone else had clearly finished. H\'f6fner was associated with lightness, improbable shapes and a mysterious ancient saint frequently depicted holding a violin-shaped bass.\
By the time the Order appeared, all three were considered so last Hootenanny.\
Nobody knew what a Hootenanny was.\
Centuries of theological scholarship produced competing accounts involving communal singing, ecstatic dancing, ritual combat, animal sacrifice and competitive knitwear. None acquired enough evidence to eliminate the others.\
The Order of the Perpetual Drop emerged when a group of purists decided that mainstream quake-trance had become spiritually empty.\
Everyone else assumed the Order was performance art.\
The Order took this as persecution.\
Its theology organised the Drop around the three principal gods and, eventually, three industrial Gyges discs.\
Ludwig received the first disc and established the pulse.\
Zildjian received the second and introduced instability into the field relationships.\
Rickenbacker received the third, tuned down towards the lowest interaction the operators could sustain without attracting either Fridge Force or structural engineers.\
The three-disc service developed slowly. The audible music established itself first, then the stasis frequencies entered beneath it, each disc adjusting against the others until the surrounding environment began to respond.\
The Order called the final state the Perpetual Drop.\
The name expressed a doctrine that the vibration continued outward long after human senses had lost it. Once Rickenbacker accepted a Drop, it passed beneath the world indefinitely, waiting for something sufficiently sympathetic to answer.\
For most of the Order\'92s history this was a harmless metaphor.\
Then quake-trance became fashionable.\
Tourists arrived first, as tourists generally do when they hear that something authentic is happening somewhere uncomfortable.\
Entrepreneurs followed because tourists seldom remain uncomfortable for free.\
The Southwest acquired resorts offering 
\f2\b Authentic Perpetual Drop Experiences
\f0\b0 , whose authenticity could be selected at booking from Standard, Premium and Foundational. Guests received ceremonial ear protection, vibration-adjusted chakra alignment and photographs beside decorative Gyges discs whose nearest connection to stasis technology was the refrigerator behind the gift-shop counter.\
Canyon landlords discovered resonance premiums.\
Wellness companies produced couples packages.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f2\b \cf0 Ludwig for Lovers
\f0\b0  was briefly successful.\

\f2\b Find Your Inner Rickenbacker
\f0\b0  survived much longer than anyone expected.\
A resort near Joshua Tree guaranteed a Spiritual Tremor every forty-five minutes, weather permitting. Another offered Zero-Risk Tectonic Spirituality, which achieved excellent customer satisfaction by mounting hydraulic shakers beneath the meditation floor and keeping its insurers entirely unaware of the terminology used in the brochure.\
The Order complained that sacred experience was being commodified.\
Its members continued attending festivals where their merchandise sold exceptionally well.\
This tension remained manageable until a luxury retreat chain registered 
\f2\b THE DROP WITHIN\'99
\f0\b0  as an international trademark.\
There followed three days of theological deliberation, two schisms, one assault involving a ceremonial cymbal and an argument over whether commercially bottled mineral water could participate in a geological sacrament.\
The Order declared the American desert 
\f2\b spiritually overdeveloped
\f0\b0 .\
It needed somewhere the gods had yet to be franchised.\
It found Goa.\
History has a taste for circles, particularly the ones nobody inside them can see.\
Psychedelic trance itself had distant roots in the Goa trance scene of the twentieth century. The genealogy had been almost completely lost by the twenty-third. Quake-trance had crossed centuries and continents, forgotten where some of its oldest musical instincts came from, reinvented itself in American canyons and then congratulated itself on discovering Goa.\
Nobody in the Order appears to have noticed.\
The property which attracted them was a bankrupt trance ashram whose previous proprietors had failed to monetise enlightenment. It had sea views, a large performance chamber, substantial electrical infrastructure and neighbours whose exposure to generations of trance culture had left them with little emotional capacity for further complaint.\
It also had water.\
Traditionalists hated this.\
Orthodox quake-trance had grown up with dry rock, hard canyon surfaces and environmental responses which could be pointed at. Goa provided saturated laterite, groundwater, tidal channels, estuarine mud, monsoon rain and the Arabian Sea.\
The senior clergy solved the doctrinal problem by announcing 
\f2\b Wet Quake-Trance
\f0\b0 .\
Older devotees called it heresy.\
Then the first season sold out.\
The difference became apparent almost immediately.\
A canyon behaved like a sounding board. Energy entered exposed rock, bounced around, shook things and generally remained close enough to the performance for everybody to congratulate themselves.\
Goa behaved like an enormous coupled membrane.\
The saturated laterite passed vibration into groundwater. The groundwater communicated with mud and tidal channels. The tidal channels had the Arabian Sea available whenever they felt ambitious.\
The first Wet Drops were extraordinary.\
Floors moved gently beneath people who had become accustomed to considerably rougher treatment from American geology. Coconut palms shivered during still evenings. Standing water developed concentric ripples whose centres could not always be located. Glasses travelled across tables. On several occasions the estuary produced small waves heading in directions which ordinary water had never previously considered.\
The congregation loved it.\
Dry quake-trance had made the earth answer.\
Wet quake-trance appeared to make the answer arrive from somewhere else.\
This was assigned to Rickenbacker.\
Attendance rose.\
The power supplied to the discs rose with it.\
Eventually the Order announced a major service dedicated to demonstrating the spiritual unity of Ludwig, Zildjian and Rickenbacker.\
Three industrial Gyges discs were installed around the performance chamber, each in a fixed mounting.\
One for Ludwig.\
One for Zildjian.\
One for Rickenbacker.\
The service began after sunset.\
People who survived it remembered the first hours as unusually good rather than historically important. Ludwig established a pulse strong enough to pass through the floor. Zildjian joined and the building began supplying its own faint percussion. When Rickenbacker entered, the low field interaction spread out into the wet ground and the landscape began to participate.\
Palms moved.\
Water rippled.\
People at the edge of the property felt vibration through their shoes.\
Farther away, dogs became interested in the ground.\
The congregation took all of this as encouragement.\
The operators pushed the three fields closer together.\
For a short period the discs achieved a degree of coherence no previous Order service had sustained.\
The room acquired a depth of vibration which several witnesses later struggled to describe. One said it felt as though the planet had become hollow. Another said the universe appeared to be clearing its throat.\
The Order regarded it as the finest Drop ever performed.\
Goa had a memorable evening.\
Several thousand kilometres away, Rotherhithe slid into the Thames.\
There was no stasis plant at Rotherhithe, no experimental field apparatus, no relevant industrial installation and no local event which made the damage intelligible. Buildings shifted. Foundations failed. Sections of riverbank moved, and then the Thames developed a hump large enough to inconvenience navigation and embarrass fluid mechanics.\
For several minutes, water accumulated above the surrounding water and remained there.\
Boats leaned on either side of it.\
Two tide gauges recorded high tide in the same stretch of river at once.\
The Port Authority discarded one reading because its software permitted only one Thames.\
Then the hump collapsed and a considerable quantity of water sought a lower administrative classification.\
Parts of Rotherhithe went with it.\
A sightseeing boat ended on the roof of a riverside car park, where its automated commentary continued because the ticket terms contained no provision for geological ascension.\
The Order heard about Rotherhithe several hours later and recognised Rickenbacker immediately.\
The British authorities were slower.\
Fridge Force was slower still.\
The suggestion that three pieces of stasis equipment participating in a religious rave in Goa had damaged London carried disadvantages as an investigative hypothesis. It offended common sense, jurisdictional boundaries and several senior officials who had already briefed governments on entirely different explanations.\
Proving it took almost a year.\
Investigators correlated surviving Goa telemetry with field signatures recorded around Rotherhithe, then reconstructed the relationship between the three generating fields. The same variables kept returning: relative phase, geometry and coherence. Distance contributed remarkably little to the result.\
The three fixed fields in Goa had behaved as one system.\
A second concentration of that system had manifested around Rotherhithe.\
Fridge Force called it a 
\f2\b remote resonance locus
\f0\b0 .\
Rotherhithe had been sitting inside it.\
That conclusion survived several attempts by committees to replace it with terminology containing more syllables.\
The final report changed stasis safety practice for generations, although the mathematics behind it remained uncomfortable. Engineers could reconstruct the Goa state and demonstrate that modest changes in phase or geometry moved the calculated region of maximum effect. Predicting the location of a new locus before an event remained deeply unreliable and eventually led to a speculative discipline called multi-universal calculus, whose main achievement for many years was persuading other physicists that its practitioners should receive less funding.\
Buried near the back of the classified technical appendices was an anomaly which had almost been discarded with the rest of the telemetry noise.\
In the final fraction of a second before coherence failed at Goa, three independent instruments recorded a signal returning through the generating array.\
It matched the signal the array itself was producing closely enough to rule out coincidence.\
The phase was reversed.\
The amplitude was slightly greater.\
The clocks presented the real problem.\
The return signal arrived before the corresponding source signal had been generated.\
Repeated reconstruction produced the same result.\
During that interval, the calculated remote resonance locus briefly coincided with the generating apparatus in Goa.\
The appendix author gave the condition a name.\

\f2\b Closed resonance.
\f0\b0 \
The three Goa discs had been mounted in fixed positions, and the condition disappeared almost immediately as their coherence collapsed. The author could find no practical means of sustaining it.\
The final paragraph offered one warning.\
A system in which the relative geometry of the generating fields could change continuously while coherence was maintained might create conditions the fixed Goa apparatus could never sustain.\
